<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">What&#8217;s new</h1><p class="ref-lede">What the Institute has added and changed, newest first, and every page that appeared in the last 30 days. Follow it with the <a href="/whats-new.xml">feed</a>.</p></header>
<section class="wn-notes" aria-labelledby="wn-notes-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="wn-notes-t">Release notes</h2><ol class="wn-list"><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-04">4 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">120 more History Connections puzzles</h3><p class="wn-s">Daily Connections now runs to the first of July 2027: 120 new puzzles from every region and age, from the Kings of Rome and the Khagans of the Göktürks to the Obas of Benin, the Māori King movement and the hidden words of Hide a vegetable.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/games#conn">History Connections</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-04">4 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">111 more encyclopedia entries, and dates for 67 events</h3><p class="wn-s">The composers, singers and writers behind Songs in History, from Josquin, Palestrina and Purcell to Paul Robeson, Pete Seeger, Miriam Makeba, Umm Kulthum, Lata Mangeshkar and Kazi Nazrul Islam; the revolutions of 1848 and the division of Korea; and the last names of the social ladders: the nobles of the robe and of the sword, lettres de cachet, the kisaeng and chungin of Joseon, the Byzantine tagmata, the Spartan mothakes and the conspiracy of Cinadon, the Khongirad and the Radhanites. Sixty-seven great events that had no dates now have them, from the Punic Wars and the Greco-Persian Wars to Waterloo and both world wars, so they stand on their century and year pages.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/encyclopedia/">Encyclopedia</a> <a href="/songs/">Songs in History</a> <a href="/societies/">Social ladders</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-04">4 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">184 more encyclopedia entries</h3><p class="wn-s">The ranks, offices and people the social ladders named: the Abbasid jurists Abu Hanifa, al-Shafiʻi and Ahmad ibn Hanbal and the Mihna; the askeri, the Enderun School and Hürrem Sultan; the taille, the gabelle, Turgot, Necker and the Women’s March on Versailles; the affranchis, Bois Caïman and Dutty Boukman; the gwageo, the hopae and the sangmin; the kuge, the hatamoto and the rōnin; the hersir, the housecarls and the Thing; Li Qingzhao, Fan Zhongyan and Ouyang Xiu; Kamehameha II and the ʻAi Noa.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/encyclopedia/">Encyclopedia</a> <a href="/societies/">Social ladders</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-03">3 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">137 more encyclopedia entries</h3><p class="wn-s">Ancient Egypt, Greece, the Maya, the Inca, the Aztecs and Hawaii as the social ladders describe them: Tutankhamun, Nefertari, the Valley of the Kings and Deir el-Medina; Athenian democracy, the boule, the agoge and Cynisca; Copán, Palenque and Calakmul; the calmecac, the mitma and the kapu. With them the people and works the profiles named: Claude Monet, Bertrand Russell, Rashi, Hildegard of Bingen, Thomas Becket, Ban Zhao, the Book of Han, the Manusmriti, the Great Purge and the Ten Commandments.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/encyclopedia/">Encyclopedia</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-03">3 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">107 new encyclopedia entries</h3><p class="wn-s">The subjects the profiles and speeches named but the encyclopedia lacked: Friedrich Engels, Harry S. Truman, Willy Brandt, Thomas More, Max Weber, Hannah Arendt, Rosa Luxemburg and Sayyid Qutb; the Paris Commune, the Nika riots, the Investiture Controversy and the July Revolution; the Dead Sea Scrolls, the Talmud, the Guru Granth Sahib and The Spirit of the Laws; the Muslim Brotherhood, the Bolsheviks and Young Italy; and many more, each written from its source and linked from the profiles that name it.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea-scrolls">Dead Sea Scrolls</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/nika-riots">Nika riots</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-03">3 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Ideologies: a new kind of profile</h3><p class="wn-s">Twenty ideologies, each with its origins, core ideas, spread and variants, its record in power, the critiques made of it and its legacy: liberalism, conservatism, republicanism and nationalism; capitalism, mercantilism and imperialism; socialism, communism, anarchism, social democracy and Maoism; fascism; Legalism in ancient China; Kemalism, pan-Arabism, Hindutva, Islamism and Peronism; and feminism. Drawn from saved sources and checked line by line, with what was done in each one’s name stated plainly.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=ideology">Ideologies</a> <a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism</a> <a href="/ideology/legalism">Legalism</a> <a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-03">3 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Twenty more movements</h3><p class="wn-s">Twenty movements, reforms and revolutions: the Counter-Reformation, Chartism, the Luddites and the Decembrists, Impressionism, Modernism and the Bauhaus, Solidarity, the Velvet Revolution and the environmental movement, the Taiping Rebellion, the May Fourth Movement, the Meiji Restoration and the Tanzimat, the Young Turks, Zionism and the Arab Spring, the Harlem Renaissance, Négritude and the Non-Aligned Movement.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=movement">Movements</a> <a href="/movement/chartism">Chartism</a> <a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> <a href="/movement/negritude">Négritude</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-03">3 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Twelve more religions and sixteen more languages</h3><p class="wn-s">Twelve religions with their spread on the faith maps: the religions of Mesopotamia, Canaan, Rome and the Celts, Slavic paganism and Mithraism, Tengrism, Bon and Chinese folk religion, and the Druze faith, Yazidism and Haitian Vodou. And sixteen languages: Pali, Tamil, Malay, Mongolian, Armenian, Georgian, Middle Persian, Ottoman Turkish, Old English, Gothic, Old Church Slavonic, Phoenician, Coptic, Ge'ez, Guarani and Hawaiian.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/faiths">Faith maps</a> <a href="/profiles?kind=religion">Religions</a> <a href="/profiles?kind=language">Languages</a> <a href="/religion/yazidism">Yazidism</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-03">3 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Twenty more cultures</h3><p class="wn-s">Profiles of twenty more peoples and cultures without a state of their own: living peoples (the San, Khoekhoe and Tuareg, the Inuit, Navajo, Lakota and Kalinago, and Aboriginal Australians) and the cultures known from archaeology, from the Clovis and Paracas cultures to La Tène, Hallstatt, Cucuteni–Trypillia, the Nuragic civilisation and the Iberians, and in Asia the Tocharians, Longshan, Hongshan, Erlitou and the Hoabinhian.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=culture">Cultures</a> <a href="/culture/san-people">San people</a> <a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians</a> <a href="/culture/nuragic-civilisation">Nuragic civilisation</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-03">3 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Eighty-one more state profiles</h3><p class="wn-s">Full profiles, with power curves, key facts, section pictures and gold links, for 81 more states of the register: from Ur, Lagash and the Old Kingdom of Egypt to the Qin, Sui and Northern Wei, the Huns, Hephthalites and Uyghurs, Phrygia, Colchis and Artaxiad Armenia, the Visigoths, Vandals and Great Moravia, the Aghlabids, Idrisids, Marinids and Zengids, Funan, Chenla, Champa, Medang and Malacca, Ife, Dagbon, Loango, Ifat and Alodia, Saint-Domingue, Colonial Brazil, the Republic of Texas and the Confederacy, and the modern republics from Hungary and Turkey to Rwanda, Singapore and East Timor.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles">Profiles</a> <a href="/empire/ur">Ur</a> <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon</a> <a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-02">2 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Two new essays: Before the State, and How Songs Travel</h3><p class="wn-s">Research Series 07 asks how peoples lived without kings, from the Natufians and the Jōmon to the five living cultures that have outlived the states around them; 08 follows the 300 songs of Songs in History from the oldest tablets to the anthems, the tunes that branched into other nations' songs, and what states and movements made of them. Also new in Songs in History: Chollima on the Wing, the song of North Korea's Chollima Movement, and an entry for the movement itself.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/essays/before-the-state">Before the State</a> <a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a> <a href="/research-series">Research Series</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-02">2 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Songs of states and causes, and a dozen new features</h3><p class="wn-s">Sixty-six songs of states, parties and causes join Songs in History, from Rule, Britannia! and the Soviet anthem to the songs of Mao's China, North Korea, fascist Italy and the Hong Kong protests, each told as history. New: compare any two profiles side by side; a timeline view of the profiles; Where Is This Song From?, a daily song game; Born on This Day on the home page; Next to read; search inside the songs' words; the encyclopedia filtered by era and by picture; levels in My Passport; an On This Day card for other websites; reading paths read aloud as a guided tour; this What's new page; worksheets on the cultures and the songs; and the Epic of Gilgamesh now in Sumerian.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/songs/">The songs</a> <a href="/compare">Compare two profiles</a> <a href="/games#songq">The song game</a> <a href="/teachers/">Worksheets</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-02">2 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Encyclopedia, offline reading, Meanwhile in the world</h3><p class="wn-s">229 songs and 16 forms of government have entries of their own; entries and songs show what else was happening in the world that year; the home page has a Song of the Day; pages already read open without a connection, and the site can be added to a phone's home screen.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/songs/">The songs</a> <a href="/governance">Forms of government</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-02">2 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Thirty new cultures</h3><p class="wn-s">The culture archive grows from 15 to 45, with more of Asia and South Asia: Mehrgarh, the Vedic civilisation, Sangam-age Tamilakam, Jōmon, Yayoi, Liangzhu, Sanxingdui, Dong Son, the Ainu and more, from the Natufians and the Yamnaya to the Taíno, the Thule people and the Māori.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=culture">Cultures</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-02">2 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">The Epic of Gilgamesh, and better performances</h3><p class="wn-s">The Epic of Gilgamesh joins Songs in History with Peter Pringle's performance in Sumerian; eight songs have a better recording.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/songs/epic-of-gilgamesh">The Epic of Gilgamesh</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-01">1 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Songs in History</h3><p class="wn-s">233 songs handed down through history, from a Sumerian hymn to songs of the Cold War: their story, their words in the original, romanized and in English where they are in the public domain, and a sung performance.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/songs/">Songs in History</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-10-31">31 October 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Forms of government</h3><p class="wn-s">Twenty forms of government, from absolute monarchy to the city-state and colonial rule, each with how it works, its history, its strengths and weaknesses; a hub that compares them and lets you design a state; and an essay, Who Should Rule?</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/governance">Forms of government</a> <a href="/essays/who-should-rule">The essay</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-10-31">31 October 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">The inventions tree</h3><p class="wn-s">218 inventions and discoveries in eleven lanes, from stone tools to the World Wide Web: what each built on, what it led to and who made it first.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/inventions">Inventions tree</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-10-30">30 October 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Social ladders</h3><p class="wn-s">How thirty societies ranked their people, ruler to enslaved, with how many stood on each rung; be born into one by chance, compare two, or put the ranks in order in a game.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/societies/">Social ladders</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-10-29">29 October 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Thirty cultures and movements, speeches and paths</h3><p class="wn-s">The first cultures and movements, 138 new encyclopedia entries, twenty more great speeches and ten more reading paths.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/speeches/">Great speeches</a> <a href="/paths/">Reading paths</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-10-28">28 October 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Rise and Fall Race, Flashcards, Who Said It?</h3><p class="wn-s">Race empires through the centuries, learn with flashcards, guess who said a quotation, test yourself at the end of a profile, and see everything you have read on My timeline.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/race">Rise and Fall Race</a> <a href="/flashcards">Flashcards</a> <a href="/games">Games</a></p></li></ol></section>
